course: dictionary
lesson: 296
title: The little word an
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «An» is a tiny word that goes before a word starting with a vowel sound, like «an apple», «an egg» or «an hour». It means one. Before a consonant sound we use «a», as in «a dog» or «a tree».
explain: Look at the sound, not just the letter. «Hour» starts with a vowel sound because the h is silent, so we say «an hour». «Unicorn» starts with a y sound, which is a consonant, so we say «a unicorn».
explain: «An» can also mean «each» or «per», especially when we talk about rates. «The bus goes 60 km an hour» means it goes 60 km each hour. «I eat an apple a day» means one apple each day.
fact: «An» means one, and it goes before a word that starts with a vowel sound.
fact: Use «a» before a consonant sound, and «an» before a vowel sound: «a cat», «an owl».
fact: In rates, «an» means «each» or «per»: «5 dollars an hour» means 5 dollars for each hour.
fact: In quick speech people often say «a apple», but in writing you must write «an apple».
fact: «An'» or «'n'» is a short form of «and», as in «fish 'n' chips»; it is a different word. Some old formal writers use «an historic day», but most people now write «a historic day».
exercise: She ate ___ egg for breakfast. | and | a | the | *an
exercise: In «The bus goes 60 km an hour», what does an mean? | Quickly | *Each, or per | Only one, like a | Before a vowel sound
exercise: Which sentence is correct? | I saw and owl in the tree. | *I saw an owl in the tree. | I saw a owl in the tree. | I saw the owl an tree.
test: Which word goes before «elephant»? | *an | and | a | the
test: Sam writes «a hour» in his diary. Is that right? | Yes, if he is talking fast | No, write an before every word | *No, write «an hour» because hour starts with a vowel sound | Yes, always
exam: Which sentence uses an correctly? | She ate an dog for a snack. | She ate a orange for a snack. | *She ate an orange for a snack. | She ate and orange for a snack.
